[[!meta title="Customizing `about:tor`"]]

# Development process

Here is how to test changes to `about:tor`, without having to rebuild Tor
Browser or Tails.

However, please consider
[Building Tor Browser](https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/applications/wiki/-/blob/master/Development-Information/Tor-Browser/dev-Build.md):
it is actually pretty easy and once done once (for a given version),
`make && make run` is a quicker & more convenient to way to iterate on
`about:tor` contents and JS code changes than what's described below.

## Initial setup

What follows assumes:

 - The current working directory is the root directory of an extracted Tor Browser tarball.
 - `sudo apt install 7zip`

## Modify files

### Page contents (HTML, JS, CSS, images)

1. Extract the files:

           7z x Browser/browser/omni.ja chrome/browser/content/browser/abouttor/

2. Modify the files in `./chrome/browser/content/browser/abouttor/`

3. Re-inject the modified files into the archive:

           7z a -mtc=off -tzip -- Browser/browser/omni.ja \
               chrome/browser/content/browser/abouttor/

4. Start Tor Browser, test, go back to step 2, rinse and repeat.

In Git, these files live in `browser/components/abouttor/content/`.

### User-visible strings

1. Extract the files:

           7z x Browser/omni.ja localization/en-US/toolkit/global/tor-browser.ftl

2. Modify `./localization/en-US/toolkit/global/tor-browser.ftl`

3. Re-inject the modified file into the archive:

           7z a -mtc=off -tzip -- Browser/omni.ja \
               localization/en-US/toolkit/global/tor-browser.ftl

4. Start Tor Browser, test, go back to step 2, rinse and repeat.

In Git, this file lives in `toolkit/locales/en-US/toolkit/global/tor-browser.ftl`.

## Submit your changes upstream

[Contributing to Tor Browser](https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/applications/wiki/-/blob/master/Development-Information/Tor-Browser/Contributing-to-Tor-Browser.md?ref_type=heads):
explains how to create issues and MRs, how to choose branch names, how to write commit messages, etc.

# Tips and resources

- To display contents under conditions that are more complex than "are we on
  Tails", e.g. during a specific time span, use `potentiallyShow` in
  `aboutTor.js`. Examples:
   - Outside of a YEC, the code has a simple example for a survey.
   - [Full blown YEC example](https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/applications/tor-browser/-/merge_requests/1689/diffs)
